<exercises xml:id="exercises-det-by-row-red" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises</title>

<!-- Exercise group -->
<xi:include href="./exercises.d/recognize-det-0.ptx" />

<!-- Exercise group -->
<xi:include href="./exercises.d/compute-by-row-red.ptx" />

<!-- Exercise group -->
<xi:include href="./exercises.d/eigenvalues.ptx" />

<exercise xml:id="exercise-det-by-row-red-vandermonde">
	<title>Determinant of a Vandermonde matrix</title>

	<introduction><p>
		Recall that in a polynomial interpolation problem,
		we need <m>n + 1</m> data points to uniquely determine a degree-<m>n</m> polynomial.
		(See <xref ref="subsection-applications-examples-poly" /> and <xref ref="theorem-applications-vandermonde" />.)
		And in that case,
		the associated <xref ref="definition-applications-vandermonde" text="title" />
		(the coefficient matrix of the polynomial interpolation problem)
		is square:
		<me>
			\begin{bmatrix}
				   1   \amp     x_1    \amp    x_1^2    \amp \cdots \amp    x_1^n    \\
				   1   \amp     x_2    \amp    x_2^2    \amp \cdots \amp    x_2^n    \\
				\vdots \amp   \vdots   \amp    \vdots   \amp        \amp    \vdots   \\
				   1   \amp x_{n + 1}  \amp x_{n + 1}^2 \amp \cdots \amp x_{n + 1}^n
			\end{bmatrix}
		</me>.
	</p></introduction>

	<task xml:id="exercise-det-by-row-red-vandermonde-2x2"><p>
		Compute the determinant of the <m>2 \times 2</m> Vandermonde matrix
		<me>
			\begin{bmatrix}
				1 \amp x_1 \\
				1 \amp x_2
			\end{bmatrix}
		</me>,
		expressed as a formula in <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>.
	</p></task>

	<task xml:id="exercise-det-by-row-red-vandermonde-3x3">

		<statement><p>
			Compute the determinant of the <m>3 \times 3</m> Vandermonde matrix
			<me>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					1 \amp x_1 \amp x_1^2 \\
					1 \amp x_2 \amp x_2^2 \\
					1 \amp x_3 \amp x_3^2
				\end{bmatrix}
			</me>,
			expressed as a formula in <m>x_1,x_2,x_3</m>.
			Simplify your expression by factoring instead of expanding.
		</p></statement>

		<hint><p>
			Begin by performing two simplifying row operations,
			then carry out a cofactor expansion.
			When simplifying,
			recall the <term>difference of squares</term> factorization formula:
			<me> a^2 - b^2 = (a - b) (a + b) </me>.
		</p></hint>

	</task>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Based on your answers
			to <xref ref="exercise-det-by-row-red-vandermonde-2x2" text="type-local" />
			and <xref ref="exercise-det-by-row-red-vandermonde-3x3" text="type-local" />,
			conjecture a formula for the determinant of the <m>4 \times 4</m> Vandermonde matrix
			<me>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					1 \amp x_1 \amp x_1^2 \amp x_1^3 \\
					1 \amp x_2 \amp x_2^2 \amp x_2^3 \\
					1 \amp x_3 \amp x_3^2 \amp x_3^3 \\
					1 \amp x_4 \amp x_4^2 \amp x_4^3
				\end{bmatrix}
			</me>.
			Then attempt to verify your conjectured formula through calculation.
		</p></statement>

		<hint><p>
			You may find it easier to compute the determinant of the transpose of the matrix
			(see <xref ref="lemma-det-by-row-red-det-transpose" />).
			When choosing simplifying row operations,
			and when attempting to simplify your final determinant calculation into factored form,
			you should find the following <term>difference of like powers</term> factorization formulas useful:
			<md>
				<mrow> a^2 - b^2 \amp = (a - b) (a + b) </mrow>
				<mrow> a^3 - b^3 \amp = (a - b) (a^2 + a b + b^2) </mrow>
			</md>.
		</p></hint>

	</task>

</exercise>

</exercises>
